  "baseline": "Both kings are castled and the material remains equal. The central pawns remain in contact on the c-file and the d-file. White can develop the remaining bishop with Bd2 while retaining central tension. White can also play b3 to prepare Bb2 without changing the central contact. The cxd5 exchange allows either exd5 or Nxd5 for Black with different structural consequences. After cxd5 exd5 Black has pawns on c5 and d5 rather than the original d5 and e6 pairing. After cxd5 Nxd5 the f6 knight occupies d5 and leaves f6 vacant. White's d3 bishop already looks toward h7 along the diagonal. The c3 knight also attacks d5 from its present square. The f3 knight also supports the pawn on d4. Black's c5 pawn still attacks White's pawn on d4. Both bishop plans leave central exchanges as options for Black. These alternatives deserve comparison rather than an assumption of a forced gain.",
